PLAIN-SPEAK QUESTION

Earlier,
we gave a weak core
a fence and a recruiting voice.

Both worked locally,
but the core itself
was falling apart too fast.

Since then,
A43 and A45 taught
the core to stay together
much better.

So A49 asks
the old question again
under a genuinely different organism.

When the surviving core
is under attack:

can it keep nearby
uncertain cells
from joining the opposition?

can it actively pull
some of those uncertain cells
back toward itself?

and do those two behaviors
finally work together
now that the core
is strong enough
to remain present
through the attack?

This is not
more refractory tuning.

It is not
stronger memory.

It is the next macro question:

CAN A SURVIVING CORE
CONTROL THE POPULATION
AROUND IT

WHILE IT IS UNDER CHALLENGE?

